<Article slug="potters-to-know">
  <p>
    Nobody makes pots alone. Everything in this studio sits inside a long tradition of functional,
    fire-marked pottery, and these are some of the people and places I would point anyone toward
    who wants to go deeper.
  </p>

  <h2>Bernard Leach and Shoji Hamada</h2>
  <p>
    The English potter <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_Leach" rel="noopener">Bernard
    Leach</a> and the Japanese potter
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sh%C5%8Dji_Hamada" rel="noopener">Shoji Hamada</a>
    started a pottery together in St Ives, Cornwall, in 1920. Between them they did more than
    anyone to bring the idea of the thoughtful, handmade, everyday pot to Europe and America.
    Hamada later returned to Mashiko in Japan and was named a Living National Treasure.
  </p>

  <h2>Mingei, the beauty of useful things</h2>
  <p>
    Leach and Hamada were close to the
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mingei" rel="noopener">Mingei</a> movement, founded in
    Japan in the 1920s by the thinker Yanagi Soetsu. Mingei means something like "art of the
    people": the idea that ordinary objects made by hand for daily use can be as beautiful as
    anything in a gallery. It is still the best short answer to why a handmade mug is worth it.
  </p>

  <h2>Warren MacKenzie</h2>
  <p>
    The American potter
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warren_MacKenzie" rel="noopener">Warren MacKenzie</a>
    trained at the Leach Pottery and spent decades in Minnesota making and teaching. He was known
    for keeping his prices low so his pots would be used, not just collected, and his influence
    on American studio pottery is enormous.
  </p>

  <h2>Gail Nichols and soda firing</h2>
  <p>
    Soda firing grew out of the much older tradition of
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salt_glaze_pottery" rel="noopener">salt glazing</a>.
    The Australian potter Gail Nichols spent years researching it, and her book
    <em>Soda, Clay and Fire</em> is the reference most soda potters, me included, have on the
    shelf. If you want to understand what happens inside the kiln, start there, or with my own
    <a href="/learn/soda-firing/">short version</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to see great pots in Southern California</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.amoca.org/" rel="noopener">AMOCA, the American Museum of Ceramic
      Art</a>, in Pomona, one of the largest museums in the country devoted to ceramics.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://mingei.org/" rel="noopener">Mingei International Museum</a>, in Balboa
      Park, San Diego, dedicated to folk art, craft and design from around the world.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Local craft fairs, studio sales and community clay studios are worth seeking out too. Buying
    directly from a potter is the best way to keep this tradition alive.
  </p>
</Article>
